    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Answer\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet. You should negotiate.**\n\nThis is an **above-average opening offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager, but as a happily employed Meta IC6 who was headhunted, **you have maximum leverage**. Lateral moves between Meta and Google (IC6 $\\leftrightarrow$ L6) carry switching risk, performance review resets, and unvested equity trade-offs. Google\u2019s current offer does not sufficiently compensate you for that switch\u2014especially given the absence of a sign-on bonus and room left in their compensation bands.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & Critical Mechanics\n\n```\nGoogle L6 Offer Breakdown:\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 Component           \u2502 Offer Value              \u2502 Market Band (Bay Area)   \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 Base Salary         \u2502 $235,000                 \u2502 $183,000 \u2013 $271,000      \u2502\n\u2502 Target Bonus (20%)  \u2502 $47,000                  \u2502 Fixed standard           \u2502\n\u2502 Equity (4-Year RSU) \u2502 $1,080,000 ($270k/yr avg)\u2502 $850k \u2013 $1.35M+          \u2502\n\u2502 Sign-on Bonus       \u2502 $0                       \u2502 $0 \u2013 $100k+              \u2502\n\u2502 4-Year Average TC   \u2502 ~$552,000 / year         \u2502 Mid-to-Upper L6 Band     \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n#### 1. Understand Google\u2019s Vesting Schedule (The Front-Loading Trap)\nGoogle typically vests initial RSUs on a **front-loaded schedule** (usually **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** vesting monthly):\n\n* **Year 1 Equity:** ~$356,000 \u2013 $410,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Year 1 TC:** ~$638,000 \u2013 $692,000\n* **Year 2 Equity:** ~$345,000 \u2013 $356,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Year 2 TC:** ~$627,000 \u2013 $638,000\n* **Year 3 Equity:** ~$216,000 \u2013 $237,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Year 3 TC:** ~$498,000 \u2013 $519,000\n* **Year 4 Equity:** ~$108,000 \u2013 $129,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Year 4 TC:** ~$390,000 \u2013 $411,000\n\n**Why this matters:**\n* Your Year 1 compensation will look inflated, but by Years 3 and 4, your base grant drops by over 60\u201370%.\n* While Google awards annual refreshers that vest evenly over 4 years (25% per year), as the recruiter noted, **refreshes are performance-dependent and discretionary**. At Meta, your grants likely vest evenly at 25%/year with substantial refresher compounding.\n\n#### 2. Base Salary & Sign-On\n* **Base ($235k):** Placed near the ~60th percentile of their posted band ($183k\u2013$271k). There is room to push higher.\n* **Sign-on ($0):** It is standard for Google to omit a sign-on bonus in their opening offer, but they frequently use $50k\u2013$100k+ cash sign-ons to close candidates coming from Meta who leave behind unvested equity or impending bonuses.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Proposal)\n\nBecause you were passively recruited and are happy in your current role, your anchor should reflect what it genuinely takes to justify walking away from Meta.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Pushes toward the upper end of the $271k salary band. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | Protects against the Year 3/4 drop-off and matches top-of-band L6 PM equity. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets unvested Meta RSUs, bonus transition timing, and lateral switching friction. |\n\n---\n\n### How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nSince you do not need to leave Meta, maintain a collaborative, confident, and polite tone.\n\n#### Key Talking Points:\n1. **Highlight passive status & Meta trajectory:** You enjoy your scope, team, and compensation trajectory at Meta as an IC6.\n2. **Address unvested equity / friction:** A lateral move to another FAANG company introduces performance review resets, project ramp-up time, and unvested stock transitions.\n3. **Address the Year 3/4 cliff:** Acknowledge Google's front-loaded vesting and state that higher initial equity plus a sign-on bonus are necessary to make the 4-year expected value superior to staying at Meta.\n\n#### Negotiation Script Outline (for Recruiter Call or Email):\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending this offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the product direction at Google.*\n> \n> *As mentioned when you first reached out, I wasn't actively looking to leave Meta. I'm very well-supported here as an IC6 with a strong performance track record and upcoming equity vesting. For me to take on the risk of a lateral transition, the package needs to clearly outpace what I'm walking away from.*\n> \n> *Looking at the structure, particularly how the initial equity tapers off in years 3 and 4, I would be ready to sign if we can bridge the gap with the following:*\n> - * **Base Salary:** $255,000*\n> - * **Equity:** $1,250,000 over 4 years*\n> - * **Sign-on Bonus:** $75,000 to offset unvested equity and transition timing*\n> \n> *If Google can meet these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Next Steps Decision Rule\n* **If they meet your counter (or get close, e.g., $1.2M equity + $50k\u2013$75k sign-on + $245k+ base):** Evaluate purely on product scope, team culture, and career growth. The compensation is top-tier for L6.\n* **If they refuse to move on all fronts:** Stay at Meta. Taking an offer at the lower end of your leverage when you are happy where you are is unnecessary.",
